<Create/Edit logrotate config for example Asterisk application>
	• sudo nano /etc/logrotate.d/asterisk
	• Paste below config configuration:
	/var/log/asterisk/messages /var/log/asterisk/security /var/log/asterisk/full {
	    daily
	    missingok
	    rotate 180
	    compress
	    delaycompress
	    notifempty
	    create 0640 asterisk asterisk
	    sharedscripts
	    postrotate
	        /bin/systemctl reload asterisk >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
	    endscript
	}
	
Explanation for each systax used in file:
		• daily → rotate logs every day
		• rotate 180 → keep ~180 days of logs (≈ 6 months), older ones are deleted automatically
		• compress → gzip older logs
		• delaycompress → first rotated file isn’t compressed until the next rotation (avoids issues if Asterisk still writes)
		• notifempty → skip rotation if log is empty
		• create 0640 asterisk asterisk → create new logs with correct permissions
		• postrotate → reload Asterisk so it starts writing to the new log files
		• Missingok → If a log file doesn’t exist, don’t throw an error. Just skip it.
		
Test logrotate manually:
	• sudo logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.d/asterisk

Check if logrotate config is valid:
	• sudo logrotate --debug /etc/logrotate.d/asterisk
This will not rotate, but it will show what logrotate would do.
	
Automatic scheduling:
logrotate is already run daily by cron (via /etc/cron.daily/logrotate), so you don’t need to add anything extra.
